[Line "9.Nd2 a5: the old main line"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: other ninth moves"]
[Summary "White's knight heads for c4 and the queenside attack is a move faster. Black slows it with ...a5, regroups with ...Kh8 and ...Ng8 and sacrifices on g3."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Nd2 { [%cal Rd2c4,Rb2b4]
The knight is going to c4 once c5 has been played, to attack d6, and f3 is possible without moving it
again. On the queenside White is a tempo ahead of the 9.Ne1 lines. On the kingside the knight defends
less than it would from e1 or f3, and it blocks the bishop for the moment. } 9... a5 { [%csl Yb4]
Because White is faster, Black first makes b4 cost two preparatory moves, a3 and Rb1, and after
...axb4 he will have the a-file as well. }
( 9... Nd7?! { The straightforward race is too slow here. } 10. b4 { After ...f5 White plays c5 and Nc4
at once and d6 is under fire before Black's attack has started. } )
( 9... c5 { The other reputable system. Black closes the queenside completely and plays ...Ne8 and
...f5. It is solid, but after Rb1 and b4 White has the only open file and Black has little to bite on;
the choice of players who want a slow game. } )
10. a3 { Preparing Rb1 and b4. }
( 10. Rb1 Nd7 11. a3 { The same position by the other move order. } 11... f5 12. b4 Kh8 )
( 10. b3 { White plans Ba3 and b4 without a3. } 10... Nd7 11. Ba3 Nc5 { The knight provokes b4 and then
steps aside. } 12. b4 axb4 13. Bxb4 Na6 14. Ba3 b6 { The c5-square is secured, the bishop on a3 has moved
three times and Black continues with ...f5 and ...Nc5. Equal. } )
10... Nd7 { Now that b4 has been delayed the knight releases the f-pawn in the usual way. }
( 10... Bd7 { Kotronias's repertoire move. The idea is to answer 11.Rb1 with 11...a4, fixing the queenside
so that b4 can be taken en passant, and 11.b3 with ...c6 or ...c5. It is the choice for those who
would rather restrain White than race him. } )
11. Rb1 f5 12. b4 Kh8 { [%cal Ge7g8,Gg8f6,Gg7h6] The move that defines this line. The e7-knight has no
future on g6, because White has not played f3 and there is nothing on f4 to attack; from g8 it comes to
f6 or h6 and frees the bishop's path to h6. The king is also off the diagonal on which c5 and Qb3 would
come with check. Note that Black keeps the tension on f5: ...f4 is not played until it gains something. }
13. f3 { White secures e4. 13.Qc2 Ng8 usually comes to the same thing. } 13... Ng8 14. Qc2 Ngf6 { Both
knights now watch e4 and h5. } 15. Nb5 { [%cal Rc4c5,Rb5c7] Preparing c5. } 15... axb4 16. axb4 Nh5
{ [%cal Gh5f4] The knight threatens to settle on f4. } 17. g3 { Keeping it out, and giving Black a target. }
17... Ndf6 18. c5 Bd7 { A developing move with tempo on the knight. } 19. Rb3 { The rook defends the
knight's retreat and, along the third rank, the pawn on g3. } 19... Nxg3 { [%csl Rg3,Rh2] The thematic
sacrifice. Black cannot make progress by quiet means once g3 is covered, and White's queenside is about to
break through, so the pawn cover goes now. The quieter 19...Bh6, offering the exchange of bishops first, is
also played. } 20. hxg3 Nh5 { The second knight follows, and g3 cannot be held: ...Nxg3 and ...f4 are both
threatened. } 21. f4 { The only way to keep some lines closed. } 21... exf4 22. c6 { White counterattacks
rather than recapture. } 22... bxc6 23. dxc6 Nxg3 24. Rxg3 fxg3 25. cxd7 g2 { Karpov-Kasparov, Tilburg 1991.
Black has a rook and four pawns for three minor pieces, and one of the pawns is on g2. The game became a
famous marathon that was eventually drawn.

**Verdict:** the sacrifice on g3 is sound and gives Black at least equal chances; White's best practical
policy is to avoid it with an earlier deviation, which is why 9.Nd2 lost its place as the main line to
9.b4 and 9.Ne1. } *

[Line "Rare ninth moves: 9.Bg5, 9.Bd2, 9.Be3, 9.a4, 9.Kh1"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: other ninth moves"]
[Summary "Developing and waiting moves. Black answers with ...Nh5 or ...Ng4 where that gains time, and otherwise with the normal ...Nd7 and ...f5."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Bg5 { White is ready to
give up the bishop: after 9...h6 10.Bxf6 Bxf6 11.b4 he has gained time for the queenside and Black's
bishop pair is slow to count. That is the most common line and a perfectly good one for Black. }
( 9. Bd2 { A modest developing move that connects the rooks and prepares Rc1 and b4. } 9... Nh5 { The
knight is aimed at f4 and the f-pawn is free without the knight having had to retreat. } 10. g3 f5
11. exf5 Nxf5 { The knight is heading for d4 and the other one returns to f6. Black's pieces are active
and the weakening g3 gives him something to play against. Equal. } )
( 9. Be3 { This walks into a tempo. } 9... Ng4 10. Bd2 f5 { Black has ...f5 in with the knight actively
placed on g4, from where it drops back to f6 when asked. He is at least equal. } )
( 9. a4 { White wants a5 before deciding on anything else. } 9... a5 { With the a-pawns fixed White's
b4 is gone for good and c5 needs long preparation with b3 and Ba3. Black plays ...Nd7 and ...f5 with
an extra tempo's worth of safety on the queenside. } )
( 9. Kh1 { A waiting move with a point: after the knight leaves f6 White plays g4 and Rg1, discouraging
...f5. } 9... Nd7 { Black develops as usual and decides after seeing g4 whether to play ...f5 anyway
or to switch to ...a5 and ...Nc5; the king on h1 and the pawn on g4 are not obviously useful then. } )
9... Nh5 { [%cal Gh5f4,Gh7h6] The active choice, in keeping with the rest of the repertoire. The knight
avoids being exchanged on f6, heads for f4 and leaves the bishop on g5 with nothing to pin. } 10. Ne1
{ Uncovering the bishop against h5. } 10... Nf4 11. Nd3 Nxe2+ 12. Qxe2 h6 13. Be3 f5 { [%cal Gf5f4,Gg6g5]
**Verdict:** Black has the bishop pair and the usual kingside advance, and White's light-squared
bishop, his best defender of g2 and h3, has gone. White is quick with c5 because his knight is already
on d3. A normal Mar del Plata race with chances for both sides. } *

[Line "8.Be3: White keeps the tension"]
[Section "Eighth-move deviations"]
[Summary "White defends d4 instead of closing the centre. Black chases the bishop with ...Ng4 and ...f6 and then strikes with ...f5."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. Be3 { White avoids the Mar del
Plata by defending d4 once more. The bishop is exposed on e3, and that is what Black plays on. }
8... Ng4 { The fighting move: the bishop is hit before h3 can be played. }
( 8... Re8 { The solid alternative, and Pavlovic's recommendation. The rook makes ...exd4 a threat to e4,
so White must act, and the point is seen if he closes the centre. After 9.dxe5 dxe5 10.Qxd8 the ending
is equal but gives Black little to play for, which is why the repertoire prefers 8...Ng4. } 9. d5 Nd4
{ The knight cannot be driven away and taking it costs the e-pawn. } 10. Nxd4 exd4 11. Bxd4 Nxe4 { Black
has regained the pawn with a freer game. } )
9. Bg5 f6 { Blocking the bishop for a moment, but the pawn will go on to f5. } 10. Bc1 { White claims that
the knight on g4 and the pawn on f6 are misplaced. Black has gained two moves, and uses them. }
( 10. Bh4 { The bishop stays active and can be chased. } 10... g5 { Gaining space with tempo; the knight
will come back via h6 to f7, where it guards e5 and d6. The price is the f5-square. } 11. Bg3 Nh6 12. dxe5
( 12. d5 { The Mar del Plata structure with White's bishop outside the chain. } 12... Ne7 { Black is
ready for ...f5; the knight on h6 supports ...g4 and covers f7 and f5. } )
12... fxe5 { [%cal Gg5g4,Gc6d4] Towards the centre, opening the f-file. Black has ...g4 and ...Nd4 to
come and the bishop on g3 bites on e5. White will try c5 to open lines before Black is coordinated.
Chances are equal. } )
10... f5 { [%cal Rf5e4] Black has ...f5 in without having had to retreat the knight first, and e4 is
attacked. } 11. dxe5
( 11. Bg5 { A third visit. } 11... Qe8 { The queen steps out of the attack and keeps an eye on e5 and h5.
Black threatens ...h6 and ...f4. } )
( 11. d5 Ne7 { A Mar del Plata in which ...f5 is already played and White's bishop has gone home.
After 12.Ng5 Nf6 Black is comfortable. } )
11... Ngxe5 { [%cal Gc8f5] [%csl Ye5,Yd4] The knight returns to the centre with gain of time.

**Verdict:** after 12.exf5 Bxf5 or 12.Nxe5 Nxe5 Black's pieces are all active, he has e5 and d4 for
his knights and the f-file for his rook. White's only trump is the d5-square, and it is not enough for
an advantage. } *

[Line "8.dxe5: the delayed exchange"]
[Section "Eighth-move deviations"]
[Summary "White exchanges on e5 with the knight already committed to c6. Black trades queens, gives a bishop for the f3-knight and plants a knight on d4."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. dxe5 dxe5 9. Bg5 { The only move
with any bite: the pin threatens Nd5. }
( 9. Qxd8 Rxd8 10. Bg5 Rf8 { The rook steps out of the pin and covers f6 and f7. After 11.Nd5 Nxd5 12.cxd5
Nd4 Black has no problems. } )
9... Qxd1 { The simplest. 9...Bg4, keeping the queens on, is the alternative for those who need to win. }
10. Rfxd1 Bg4 { [%cal Gg4f3,Gc6d4] [%csl Yd4] Black intends to remove the knight that guards d4 and to
occupy the square. } 11. h3 Bxf3 12. Bxf3 Nd4 { **Verdict:** the knight on d4 is as good as either of
White's bishops, which have nothing to attack. Black covers c7 with ...c6 or ...Ne8 against Nd5 and
Nb5 and brings the rooks to the d-file. The position is equal and dry, and Black should not expect more
than half a point against accurate play. } *
